# Copy to `.env`. Loaded by main.py at startup; real environment variables win.
#
# Env holds what varies per machine: where files live, which host to talk to,
# where an external tool is, and how hard *this* machine is allowed to pull --
# rate, concurrency, quota and retry shape are properties of your link, your
# proxy and your standing with the server, not of the archive. Everything else
# about behaviour (templates, filters, timers) belongs in data/config.json,
# which is meant to be shareable.
#
#   precedence:  environment  >  data/config.json  >  built-in defaults
#
# A malformed value here raises at startup rather than falling back to the
# default: a mistyped cap must never silently become "no cap".

# ---- Proxy (standard names; requests reads these automatically) ----
# HTTP_PROXY=http://127.0.0.1:7890
# HTTPS_PROXY=http://127.0.0.1:7890
# ALL_PROXY=http://127.0.0.1:7890
# NO_PROXY=localhost,127.0.0.1

# ---- Where things live ----
# PAWCHIVE_DATA_DIR says where config.json itself is, so it can only be set
# here -- never in config.json.
# PAWCHIVE_DATA_DIR=data
# PAWCHIVE_CACHE_DIR=cache
# PAWCHIVE_LOGS_DIR=logs
# PAWCHIVE_DOWNLOAD_DIR=F:/Download/kemono

# ---- Endpoints (Pawchive has changed domains before: .st -> .pw) ----
# PAWCHIVE_API_BASE=https://pawchive.pw/api/v1
# PAWCHIVE_FILE_BASE=https://file.pawchive.pw

# ======================================================================
# Concurrency caps
# ======================================================================
# The three pool sizes MULTIPLY. artists x posts x files is how many transfers
# can be in flight at once, and that product is not a number anyone chooses on
# purpose -- 3 x 5 x 10 is 150 simultaneous connections from a config that
# looks modest on every line.
#
# PAWCHIVE_MAX_CONCURRENT_DOWNLOADS is the cap the server actually sees: one
# global ceiling on live file transfers, held regardless of how the pools above
# it are sized. Set the pools for pipeline shape, set this for politeness.
# 0 disables it and restores the old multiply-it-out behaviour.

# PAWCHIVE_MAX_CONCURRENT_ARTISTS=3
# PAWCHIVE_MAX_CONCURRENT_POSTS=1
# PAWCHIVE_MAX_CONCURRENT_FILES=2
# PAWCHIVE_MAX_CONCURRENT_DOWNLOADS=2

# ======================================================================
# Traffic caps
# ======================================================================
# Sizes accept a suffix: B, KB, MB, GB, TB (1024-based). 0 = no limit.
#
# RATE is bytes per second summed over every transfer, so it is a real ceiling
# on the line rather than a per-connection one. BURST is how much may be drawn
# at once after an idle spell; it defaults to one second of rate, because a
# bigger bucket lets a resumed run open with exactly the spike a server
# rate-limits on. Leave it alone unless you know you want the surge.
#
# QUOTA bounds a rolling window (default 24h) and is counted in data/quota.json
# so a restart cannot hand back a spent budget. When it runs out the downloader
# logs one `quota_paused` line and stops making requests entirely; nothing is
# marked failed, posts just stay undone until the window rolls.
#
# Reference points: 4MB/s saturated for a full day is ~330GB. If you are being
# asked to slow down, the quota is the setting that actually answers -- a rate
# cap alone still moves an unbounded amount given enough hours.

# PAWCHIVE_MAX_DOWNLOAD_RATE=4MB
# PAWCHIVE_DOWNLOAD_BURST=8MB
# PAWCHIVE_DAILY_DOWNLOAD_QUOTA=60GB
# PAWCHIVE_QUOTA_WINDOW_HOURS=24

# Requests per second to the file host -- a different cap from either of the
# two above, and the one Pawchive's operator actually states. A thousand small
# files sit far under any byte rate while hammering the request count, and the
# request count is what a host counts when it decides you are being rude.
# BURST is the bucket: 1 means strict spacing, larger permits a clump then a
# gap. 0 disables the cap.
#
#   The operator asks for: at most 1 request/second, at most 5 active downloads.

# PAWCHIVE_MAX_FILE_REQUESTS_PER_SECOND=1
# PAWCHIVE_FILE_REQUEST_BURST=1

# ======================================================================
# Retries and backoff
# ======================================================================
# Attempts per request. 0 = retry forever, which is deliberate for API list
# requests: losing a page truncates a creator. Downloads are bounded instead,
# because a give-up only leaves that post undone and the next run picks it up,
# whereas an unbounded wait stalls the whole queue behind one bad file.
#
# NOT_FOUND_MAX_RETRIES exists because a 404 can be one bad edge node; past
# that it is treated as permanent so a removed creator cannot stall the queue.

# PAWCHIVE_REQUEST_TIMEOUT=30
# PAWCHIVE_MAX_RETRIES=0
# PAWCHIVE_DOWNLOAD_MAX_RETRIES=4
# PAWCHIVE_NOT_FOUND_MAX_RETRIES=2

# Backoff shape: wait RETRY_DELAY, then multiply by RETRY_BACKOFF each round,
# stopping at RETRY_DELAY_CAP.
#
# RETRY_JITTER (0..1) spreads each wait by that fraction, and it matters more
# here than it looks. The workers sit behind one shared cap, so when the host
# pushes back they all fail within the same second -- with identical backoff
# they would then all return within the same second, and the burst the host was
# objecting to arrives again intact. 0 disables it.
#
# Set RETRY_BACKOFF=1 to keep the wait flat, which is the right shape when a
# host is signalling a fixed cooldown rather than asking you to yield.

# PAWCHIVE_RETRY_DELAY=10
# PAWCHIVE_RETRY_BACKOFF=2
# PAWCHIVE_RETRY_DELAY_CAP=300
# PAWCHIVE_RETRY_JITTER=0.3

# ======================================================================
# What to do about each kind of failure
# ======================================================================
# The right response to a status code is a property of the host, not of the
# code. One archive answers 403 while it warms a file and wants patience;
# another means it and wants to be left alone. So it is a table, not an
# if-ladder in the client.
#
#   syntax:  <key>=<action>[,<setting>=<value>...] ; <key>=...
#
# KEYS, matched most specific first:
#   404 4xx default          an HTTP status, its class, then the catch-all
#   timeout connection       a request that never got an answer
#   network                  either of those two, if you do not split them
#   invalid                  a well-formed reply of the wrong shape
#
# ACTIONS:
#   retry      keep trying on the ladder below
#   fail       give up now and report it. The post stays undone and the next
#              run tries again from scratch -- nothing is lost, only deferred
#   permanent  declare the resource gone. This is the one that keeps a creator
#              removed upstream from stalling an unbounded retry forever, and
#              blocking every creator queued behind it
#
# SETTINGS, each optional and each falling back to the global value above:
#   attempts   how many times THIS key may match before the action fires.
#              Counted per key, so a 404 among other errors still has to be
#              seen `attempts` times before it is believed, and a 429 ladder is
#              not advanced by unrelated 500s. Absent: `retry` inherits the
#              caller's bound (unlimited for list requests, DOWNLOAD_MAX_RETRIES
#              for files) while `fail` and `permanent` act the first time.
#              A rule's own count may exceed the caller's bound -- that is how a
#              host that wants ten patient tries on 429 gets them without
#              loosening anything else. -1 never gives up.
#   delay backoff cap jitter    this key's own ladder. backoff=1 keeps the wait
#              flat, which is what a fixed cooldown wants.
#
# Left empty, the built-in behaviour applies: 404 is permanent after
# NOT_FOUND_MAX_RETRIES tries, everything else is retried. Naming 404 here
# takes over from that setting.
#
# Some shapes worth knowing:
#   429=retry,attempts=10,delay=120,backoff=1     told to slow down: wait, flat
#   403=fail                                      told to go away: stop asking
#   403=retry,attempts=8,delay=300,backoff=1      a host that warms files slowly
#   410=permanent                                 gone means gone
#   5xx=retry,attempts=6,delay=15                 the host is having a bad day
#   timeout=retry,attempts=8;connection=retry,delay=30    flaky proxy

# PAWCHIVE_STATUS_POLICIES=429=retry,attempts=10,delay=120,backoff=1; 5xx=retry,delay=15

# ---- Misc ----
# User-Agent. Pawchive's operator asks for something identifiable and NOT a
# browser string; a browser-impersonating UA is answered with a flat 403 on
# every request that reaches the origin. Edge-cached files keep serving, so the
# symptom reads as an intermittent or partial block rather than a rejected
# header -- which is what makes it so easy to misdiagnose as a rate limit or an
# IP ban. Adding a contact address is welcomed but not required.
# Default: pawchive-downloader/1.0
# PAWCHIVE_USER_AGENT=pawchive-downloader/1.0 (+you@example.com)
# PAWCHIVE_CLOUD_DIR=cloud
# PAWCHIVE_GDOWN_BIN=gdown
